.nh
.TH gloat-install -- Installing Gloat
Gloat has \fBzero dependencies\fP - everything installs automatically on first
use.

.PP
There are two main ways to get started: cloning the repository and sourcing
the \fB\&.rc\fR file, or the quick install script.

.SH Clone and Source (Recommended)
For Bash, Fish or Zsh:

.EX
git clone https://github.com/gloathub/gloat
source gloat/.rc
gloat --help
.EE

.PP
The \fBsource gloat/.rc\fR command adds the \fBgloat\fR command to your PATH, enables
the \fBgloat*\fR man pages and sets up \fBgloat\fR tab completion.

.PP
On first run, Gloat will automatically install all required tools (Go, Glojure,
YAMLScript, Babashka, etc) to \fB\&.cache/local/\fR within the project directory.
Just run \fBgloat --help\fR once to complete the setup.

.PP
To make Gloat available permanently, simply add this to your shell's (Bash,
Zsh or Fish) rc file (\fB~/.bashrc\fR, \fB~/.config/fish/config.fish\fR or
\fB~/.zshrc\fR):

.EX
source /path/to/gloat/.rc
.EE

.PP
Yes, that command and \fB\&.rc\fR file actually supports all three shells!

.SH Quick Install
For Bash or Zsh, install \fBgloat\fR for the current shell session:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) gloat
.EE

.PP
For Fish:

.EX
curl -sL in-1.cc | source - gloat
.EE

.PP
This uses in-1, which clones itself to \fB/tmp/in-1\fR,
installs gloat and all of its dependencies under that directory, and then
adds the \fBgloat\fR command to your current \fBPATH\fR along with the \fBgloat*\fR man
pages and \fBgloat\fR tab completion.
Nothing else on your system changes.

.PP
Pass in-1 arguments after \fBgloat\fR to pin a version or choose a different
directory (this works the same in Fish):

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) gloat GLOAT-VERSION=0.1.37
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) gloat PREFIX=~/.gloat
.EE

.PP
To keep \fBgloat\fR around for good, install the \fBin-1\fR command (see
https://in-1.cc/install/) and run:

.EX
in-1 --local gloat
.EE

.PP
That installs gloat under \fB~/.local/share/gloat/<version>\fR and writes a
\fBgloat\fR wrapper into \fB~/.local/bin\fR, which works from any shell with no
further setup.

.SH Upgrading
Upgrade gloat to the latest release:

.EX
gloat --upgrade
.EE

.PP
Pin (or roll back) to a specific version:

.EX
gloat --upgrade=v1.2.3
.EE

.PP
In both cases, gloat will:
.IP "  1." 5
Switch the local checkout to the target tag.
.IP "  2." 5
Remove \fB\&.cache/\fR\&.
.IP "  3." 5
Install the core dependencies that pair with that gloat release, including
the matching GLJ version. The YAMLScript compiler is installed later if a
\fB\&.ys\fR file is compiled.

.PP
If your clone has uncommitted changes, \fB--upgrade\fR will refuse and ask you
to stash or commit them first.

.PP
If you installed via the Makefile installer, the checkout under
\fB~/.local/share/gloat\fR is detached-HEAD and upgrading there always works.

.PP
If you installed via the quick install, upgrade with in-1 instead, which
installs the release that its Makes
module currently pins, alongside the old one:

.EX
source <(curl -sL in-1.cc) -U gloat
in-1 -U --local gloat
.EE

.PP
Pass \fBGLOAT-VERSION=x.y.z\fR to pick a release yourself.

.SH Resetting the Cache
If something gets stuck or you want to force a fresh dependency install
without changing version:

.EX
gloat --reset
.EE

.PP
This removes \fB\&.cache/\fR entirely (binaries, build artifacts, REPL working
dirs). The next \fBgloat\fR invocation reinstalls everything.

.SH Uninstalling
If you used the quick install, everything lives under \fB/tmp/in-1\fR (or the
\fBPREFIX\fR you gave), so removing that directory is all it takes:

.EX
rm -rf /tmp/in-1
.EE

.PP
For an \fBin-1 --local gloat\fR install, remove the versioned directory and the
wrappers:

.EX
rm -rf ~/.local/share/gloat/<version> ~/.local/bin/gloat ~/.local/bin/gloat-*
.EE

.PP
If you used the Makefile installer:

.EX
make -f <(curl -sL gloathub.org/make) uninstall
make -f <(curl -sL gloathub.org/make) uninstall-glj   # if you installed glj
.EE

.PP
This removes the \fBgloat\fR symlink from \fB~/.local/bin\fR and the cloned tree
from \fB~/.local/share/gloat\fR (use \fBPREFIX=...\fR if you installed to a custom
prefix). After uninstalling, remove the \fBsource .../.rc\fR line you added to
your shell rc, or new shells will fail to start.

.PP
If you used the clone method, uninstalling is just removing the clone and
the \fBsource\fR line:

.EX
rm -rf /path/to/gloat
# Then remove the `source /path/to/gloat/.rc` line from your shell rc.
.EE
